# SESSION PROMPT FORGE — Creación Digital I / II

Use this forge to write an instructor-facing guide for one real 2-hour class.
It follows the prompt pattern used in the advertising-video editing course:
context, learning goals, timed opening, conceptual turn, screening/analysis,
studio practice, individual check, and follow-up. It is a teaching instrument,
not an institutional timetable and not a research protocol.

## A. Bound the session before writing

Load, in this order:

1. the matching `cv/guides/*.json` and the live-PDF URL recorded in
   `oficial-guia-framework.mdc`;
2. `cv/UNIT-PLAN.md`, `grounding/README.md`, and the unit's existing lesson;
3. the unit row in `docs/_data/tracks.yml`.

Copy CONTENIDOS phrases and competency codes verbatim. The registry's hour
cells describe the unit's 80-hour contact-forgeable allocation; they do not
turn into a fictitious week-by-week calendar. This file specifies one
120-minute meeting inside that unit slice.

Required header fields:

```yaml
course: CD I | CD II
unit_id: I.1–I.9 | II.1–II.6
official_contents: "verbatim JSON contents anchor"
competencies: [CON1, HAB9, HAB10, COMP8, COMP9] # use only course JSON codes
session_length: 120 minutes
evidence_state: SAFE | bounded SAFE | NONE | [BIBLIO-GAP]
registry_slice: "copy the unit row's official activity cells"
```

## B. The 120-minute lesson shape

Keep the full lesson at 120 minutes. A practical default is:

| Time | Move | Evidence produced |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 00–10 | Welcome, retrieval question, visual hook | oral diagnostic |
| 10–30 | Mini-lecture: one claim, one boundary, one vocabulary set | B1 notes |
| 30–45 | Screen one short video and inspect 2–3 stills | observation grid |
| 45–55 | Pause, compare, and name the professional criterion | debate / critique |
| 55–65 | Instructor demonstration or live graphic | annotated process |
| 65–100 | Studio sprint in pairs or small groups | B2 prototype |
| 100–113 | Individual problem, completed without AI first | B3 evidence |
| 113–120 | Share-out, exit ticket, next-step hand-in | formative feedback |

The studio task must be contextualised and collaborative when appropriate;
the B3 problem must be individually attributable and decontextualised. At
least one B3 item must be solvable without AI and must say so plainly.

## C. Media brief — three cards per session

Every prompt requests a small media pack, but media is selected by meaning
before decoration. Use the Profield media prospector request in
`PROFIELD-MEDIA-PROSPECTOR-REQUEST.md`.

### Video card

- `role`: demonstration, historical context, process comparison, or audience
  experience;
- `query`: field + subfield + concept + format;
- `duration`: normally 30–180 seconds, with exact start/end time;
- `studio_semantics`: what students must notice and annotate;
- `source_url`, creator, date, and `license_evidence`;
- caption/transcript, language, and a non-video fallback.

### Still-image card

- 2–3 images, each classified as exemplar, counterexample, reference, object,
  or process trace;
- `studio_semantics` and a one-sentence viewing question;
- creator, canonical source URL, stable asset URL, credit line, and licence;
- crop/transform permission and whether the image can enter the repository.

### Graphic card

- one explanatory diagram generated in-repo or sourced from a conforming
  licence;
- alt text, visible labels, and a high-contrast / text-only fallback;
- no Ahmes, Athanor, DevIAC, node IDs, local paths, or resolver labels in the
  artwork;
- if generated by the course, mark it as course content; if sourced, preserve
  the original attribution and licence.

The prospector must reject an asset when the licence is unknown, the source is
not canonical, the asset is a platform thumbnail without reuse permission, or
its studio semantics do not match the requested lesson move. Embedding a
video is preferable to downloading it. Download, crop, transcode, or remix
only when the licence explicitly permits that action.

## D. Grounding and quotation gate

This section is governed by [`PROVENANCE-LAW.mdc`](./PROVENANCE-LAW.mdc).
Every Athanor discovery and Ahmes-derived claim must carry one complete
`PROVENANCE_LINE` in the source comment; a vector preview is never a citation.

Run one scoped Athanor discovery query for the unit's field/subfield. Record
the query, source locator, similarity, and proposed use in the private forge
note. A vector/Athanor snippet is a pointer only.

For any claim that survives discovery:

```text
Athanor pointer → Ahmes extraction.db → complete node + page context
→ ahmes query --cite → evaluator_safe gate → public paraphrase/short quote
```

Use only evaluator-safe nodes in visible references. Keep the full coat, node,
page, resolver result, and discovery path in a source-only `curriculum-internal`
comment when the lesson is public. Never use a vendor manual as evidence that
a teaching sequence works.

When the row is `NONE` or `[BIBLIO-GAP]`, the prompt must say “declared pilot”
or “open teaching question” in ordinary language. Do not invent a citation,
turn an unsafe metadata record into a polished bibliography entry, or imply a
measured learning effect.

Short Ahmes quotations are allowed only after node verification and must stay
within copyright limits. Prefer a paraphrase plus one short operative phrase.

## E. Output contract

Each filled prompt must contain:

1. `Context & learning goals` — one central question and 3–5 observable goals.
2. `Session A: ... (2 hours)` — the complete timed run of show.
3. `Screening & analysis` — video, stills, graphic, and a comparison question.
4. `Studio sprint` — deliverable, materials, definition of done, accessibility.
5. `Individual problem` — 3–5 items, including one no-AI diagnostic.
6. `Media prospector request` — field, subfield, semantics, licence policy.
7. `Grounding note` — safe citation, declared gap, or both; never a vector quote.
8. `Follow-up` — process evidence, attribution, export, and next class bridge.

## F. Critical field/subfield layer — mandatory

Critical thinking must be specific to the field and subfield, not a detachable
generic “ethics” paragraph. Every filled prompt adds a section titled
`Critical field lens & open living prompt` with four parts:

1. **Field tension:** name the material, cultural, economic, ecological, bodily,
   labour, access, authorship, or platform tension created by this practice.
2. **Positioned question:** ask who benefits, who carries the cost, whose
   knowledge or body is normalised, what becomes invisible, and what evidence is
   missing. Choose the questions that actually fit the subfield.
3. **Verified perspective:** include one short quotation or careful paraphrase
   from a page-verified evaluator-safe Ahmes node. A quote from the current
   bank is available in `CRITICAL-FIELD-LAYER.md`; if the unit has no safe node,
   say so and use a declared open question instead. Never promote a vector
   preview or an unsafe record.
4. **Open living prompt:** write one question that stays open across the course.
   It must name the phenomenon, the affected relation, and the evidence students
   could bring next time. Do not resolve it with a slogan or force consensus.

Use this reusable form:

```markdown
## Critical field lens & open living prompt

**Field tension:** <specific tension in this field/subfield>.

**Verified perspective:** <short quote or paraphrase> (Author Year, page).
This supports <bounded perspective>; it does not prove <larger claim>.

**Open living prompt:** As <practice/technology> changes <relation>, who gains
control, who absorbs the cost, and what evidence would change our judgement?

**Bring forward:** one source, observation, process trace, or counterexample;
name whose perspective is still absent.
```

The instructor records the prompt’s evolution after critique. A changed answer
is not a failure: the aim is a visible history of better questions, situated
evidence, and accountable design decisions. This layer remains L1 teaching;
it is not a research instrument and must not collect student data.

Before release, run `npm run build`, `npm run verify:publication`, and
`npm run verify:citations`. Instructor answer sketches stay in `private/` and
must never enter the student page.

## G. Synergy and external naming — UEM only

Session prompts and any lesson copy they feed must **not** name UDIT, Web
Atelier, or Front-End I/II. Use neutral UEM-facing language only: **shared
digital showroom (L3)**, **fashion-design CLO workflow**, **sibling web
runtime**. Cross-institutional engineering details stay out of student-facing
and instructor-facing forge output until explicitly authorised elsewhere.
